.TH GLASSOSDRC 5 "2026-08-29" "glassosd" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
glassosdrc \- configuration file for glassosd
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I ~/.config/glassosdrc
.SH DESCRIPTION
An INI file in the KConfig style: groups in square brackets, then
.IR key = value
lines.
.PP
Every key has a
.BR glassosdctl (1)
equivalent that applies live, without a restart, and writing the file by hand
has the same effect. The exception is
.BR Modules ,
which is read once at startup.
.SH THE NOTIFICATIONS GROUP
.TP
.B Enabled
Whether popups appear at all. Default true.
.TP
.B Limit
How many popups may be on screen at once. Default 4.
.TP
.B HistoryLength
How many notifications history keeps. Default 200. Identical repeats collapse
onto one counted entry, so a repeating sender occupies one slot rather than
hundreds.
.TP
.B Timeout ", " TimeoutLow ", " TimeoutCritical
How long popups stay, in milliseconds; 0 means until dismissed. Defaults 6000,
4000 and 0. A notification's own requested timeout wins where it sets one.
.TP
.B HoverPause
Hold a popup open while the pointer is on it. Default true.
.TP
.B DoNotDisturb
Whether Do Not Disturb is on. Persists across login. Suppressed notifications
are still recorded.
.TP
.B IndicateHidden
Whether a "+N more" row is shown when the display limit holds notifications
back. Default true.
.TP
.B CoalesceThreshold ", " CoalesceWindowMs
How many notifications from one application within how long collapse into a
single card. Defaults 3 and 20000.
.TP
.B AutoCollapseOver
Group size at which the notification centre opens a group folded. Default 3.
.TP
.B IdleThresholdMs
How long without input before the daemon considers you away. Default 120000.
.TP
.B NewestFirst
Whether history lists the newest entry at the top. Default false, which puts it
at the bottom the way a message thread does, since the centre opens scrolled
there.
.SH THE APPEARANCE GROUP
.TP
.B Theme
.B dark
or
.BR light .
.TP
.B ThemeFile
Name of a theme in the search path; see
.B THEMES
below.
.TP
.B AccentColor
Overrides the theme's accent. Darkened automatically if it would fail contrast.
.TP
.B Scale
Size multiplier for all text and spacing, 0.6 to 2.0. Applied on top of the
display's own scale factor.
.TP
.B FontSize
Base point size. Wins over the theme's
.BR font.size .
.TP
.B NotifyWidth
Popup width in logical pixels, 240 to 900.
.TP
.B Solidity
Pushes every glass surface toward opaque, 0.0 to 1.0. 0 keeps the tuned glass.
Intended for compositors that do not blur, where translucency is merely
see\-through.
.TP
.B Position ", " OffsetX ", " OffsetY
Where popups sit, and extra clearance from the anchored edges. See
.B POSITION
below.
.TP
.B CentrePosition ", " OsdPosition
Which side the control centre opens on, and whether the OSD sits at the top,
centre or bottom.
.TP
.B Widgets
The control centre's contents, in order. See
.B WIDGETS
below.
.TP
.B ButtonsPerRow
Columns in the button grid. Default 5.
.TP
.B Level
.B segmented
or
.BR bar ,
for the volume and brightness indicator.
.TP
.B Sound ", " OsdSound
Notification sounds, and feedback on volume steps and lock keys. Default true
and false.
.TP
.B QuietWhileBusy
Hold notifications back while an application is asking that the screen not
blank. Default false. See
.B QUIET WHILE BUSY
below.
.SH POSITION
.nf
glassosdctl position bottom\-right
glassosdctl offset 0 160
.fi
.PP
Accepted positions are
.BR top\-left ,
.BR top\-centre ,
.BR top\-right ,
.BR bottom\-left ,
.B bottom\-centre
and
.BR bottom\-right .
.PP
Popups clear your panel or taskbar automatically, whatever its thickness and
whichever edge it is on: glassosd requests no exclusive zone of its own but
respects everyone else's, so the compositor keeps it out of the way. Nothing to
configure, and it follows the panel if you move it.
.PP
.B offset
is for what panel awareness cannot cover \(em a browser's tab strip, a video
player's controls, a game's HUD. None of those reserve an exclusive zone, so
nothing tells the compositor they are there. Two numbers, X then Y, in logical
pixels, applied only to the edges you are anchored to; one number sets both.
Zero is not flush against the screen edge, because the card shadow already
insets the stack.
.PP
The stack always grows away from its anchor, so the newest notification is the
one nearest the corner you chose, whichever corner that is.
.SH MODULES
Each half runs independently. Off means never constructed: no bus name, no
Wayland surface, no watchers. Modules are read at startup, so changing one
needs
.BR "glassosdctl restart" .
.TP
.B notifications
The daemon and its popups.
.TP
.B centre
The control centre and tray icon.
.TP
.B osd
Volume, brightness and media OSD.
.TP
.B lockkeys
Caps, num and Fn lock OSDs.
.SH RULES
A rule is a
.BI "[Rule " name ]
group. Matchers decide what it applies to; actions decide what happens. Every
set matcher must match, and patterns are globs rather than regular expressions,
matching
.BR dunst (1)
so that dunstrc entries can be copied across.
.PP
.nf
glassosdctl rule\-set signal appname=Signal set_stack_tag=chat timeout=8000
glassosdctl rule\-set noisy  appname=Steam skip_display=true
glassosdctl rules
.fi
.SS Matchers
.B appname
.RB "\(bu " summary
.RB "\(bu " body
.RB "\(bu " category
.RB "\(bu " desktop_entry
.RB "\(bu " match_urgency
.RB "\(bu " focus
.SS Actions
.TP
.B set_stack_tag
Give the notification a tag, so a later notification with the same tag replaces
it rather than stacking beside it.
.TP
.B timeout
Milliseconds on screen; 0 never expires.
.TP
.B set_urgency
0 low, 1 normal, 2 critical.
.TP
.B skip_display
No popup; still recorded. Takes true or false, and false is meaningful \(em it
puts back something an earlier rule hid, which is how a focus mode's allow list
works.
.TP
.B history_ignore
Not recorded either.
.TP
.B always_collapsed
The group opens folded in the centre.
.TP
.B sound
A sound name, a file path, or
.B none
to silence this rule. See
.BR SOUNDS .
.TP
.B focus
Apply only while that focus mode is active.
.TP
.B run
Run a command on match. See
.B "THE RUN ACTION"
below.
.TP
.B snooze
Minutes to defer by. The notification is recorded immediately and shown later:
it arrived, it is waiting, not lost.
.TP
.B repeat_window
Seconds for which a byte\-identical repeat does not reopen a popup. History
still records every copy.
.RS
.PP
.B set_stack_tag
alone is not enough for a sender that repeats indefinitely. It collapses copies
onto the card already up, but only while that card exists: once it expires, the
next copy has nothing to replace and opens a fresh popup. PowerDevil re\-sends
an identical low\-battery warning once or twice a second for as long as a device
is low, so without this you get a new card every time the last one times out.
.PP
Matched on app name, summary and body together, so a rule carrying this still
lets 12%, then 9%, then 6% through as three separate pieces of news. It is the
repeat that is suppressed, not the subject.
.RE
.SS The run action
.nf
glassosdctl rule\-set logmail appname=Thunderbird "run=/home/me/bin/log.sh %a %s"
.fi
.PP
Placeholders are
.B %a
app,
.B %s
summary,
.B %b
body,
.B %c
category,
.B %u
urgency and
.B %i
id. The same values are exported as
.BR $GLASSOSD_APP ,
.BR $GLASSOSD_SUMMARY ,
.BR $GLASSOSD_BODY ,
.BR $GLASSOSD_CATEGORY ,
.BR $GLASSOSD_URGENCY ,
.B $GLASSOSD_ID
and
.BR $GLASSOSD_DESKTOP_ENTRY .
.PP
.B The command is not run through a shell.
It is split into arguments and executed directly, and placeholders are
substituted after the split \(em so a summary reading
.I "x; rm \-rf ~"
arrives as one argument, not as a command. Anyone can send you a notification;
treating its text as shell input would be a hole.
.PP
That means shell syntax \(em pipes, redirection,
.B &&
\(em does not work inline. If you need it, call a shell explicitly and read the
.IR environment ,
never a placeholder:
.PP
.nf
.RB "# safe: the value never passes through the parser"
run=sh \-c 'echo "$GLASSOSD_SUMMARY" >> ~/notifications.log'

.RB "# unsafe: %s is substituted into a string the shell then parses"
run=sh \-c 'echo %s >> ~/notifications.log'
.fi
.SS Per-app settings in the centre
The sliders icon on a group header writes these same rules, offering four
outcomes rather than raw keys:
.TP
.B Mute
.B skip_display
\(em no popup, still kept in history.
.TP
.B Ignore
.B history_ignore
plus
.B skip_display
\(em dropped entirely.
.TP
.B "Never expire"
.B timeout=0
\(em popups stay until dismissed.
.TP
.B "Always collapsed"
.B always_collapsed
\(em the group opens folded.
.PP
Ignore holds Mute on for as long as it is set, because a notification kept
nowhere but still flashing on screen is not what "ignore" means. Turning Ignore
off clears both.
.SH FOCUS MODES
A bundle of rules and an allow or block list, switched as a unit.
.PP
.nf
glassosdctl focus\-set work   allow=Signal,Jenkins
glassosdctl focus\-set gaming block=Slack,Discord
glassosdctl focus work
glassosdctl focus off
.fi
.PP
.B allow=
silences everything except the applications named;
.B block=
silences only those. Names are matched against both the application name and
its desktop entry, since the one you know is whichever you happen to have seen.
.PP
Rules can belong to a mode, which is the more powerful half:
.PP
.nf
glassosdctl rule\-set standup focus=work appname=Calendar sound=bell
.fi
.PP
A rule carrying
.B focus=
is simply not there when that mode is off, so a mode is a bundle rather than a
set of edits to undo afterwards. Allow and block lists are compiled into rules
and appended after the hand\-written ones, so there is one engine with one set
of semantics, and a mode has the final say rather than being quietly undone by
a rule written months ago. Silenced notifications are still recorded.
.SH SOUNDS
Names are standardised by the XDG sound naming specification, and themes ship
them under
.IR /usr/share/sounds/<theme>/stereo/ ,
so glassosd asks for a name and lets your sound theme answer.
.PP
The sound is chosen by the notification's
.I category
first, since that says what the thing is, and by
.I urgency
second, since that only says how much it matters.
.TP
.B im.*
message\-new\-instant
.TP
.B email.*
message
.TP
.BR device.added / device.removed
device\-added / device\-removed
.TP
.BR network.connected / network.disconnected
network\-connectivity\-established / network\-connectivity\-lost
.TP
.BR transfer.complete / transfer.error
complete / dialog\-error
.PP
Falling through to urgency: critical is
.BR dialog\-warning ,
normal is
.BR message ,
low is silent. Only categories with a matching name in the specification are
mapped, rather than inventing names no theme ships.
.PP
.B glassosdctl sounds
lists every name your installed themes provide and which theme is in use. Worth
checking before writing a rule: the themes are not interchangeable. Freedesktop
ships 35 names; KDE's
.B ocean
adds about 30 more of its own, so a rule naming one of those is silent unless
ocean is your theme or inherits into it.
.SS Custom sounds
Two ways, and the first is better.
.TP
.B "As part of a theme"
Drop the file at
.I ~/.local/share/sounds/<theme>/stereo/<name>.oga
and use it as
.BR sound=<name> .
It then follows theme switching and inheritance like any other name, and one
name can have different files per theme.
.TP
.B "As a path"
.B sound=/path/to/ding.ogg
in a rule. No theming, but nothing to set up either.
.B ~/
is expanded. A path that does not exist is reported in the journal rather than
failing silently.
.PP
Sounds follow the popup: a muted application, Do Not Disturb and an inhibition
each silence the sound along with the card, because a notification nobody is
shown should not announce itself. Bursts are rate limited, so twelve
notifications arriving together make one sound rather than twelve.
.PP
Played through
.BR canberra\-gtk\-play (1),
which resolves the name against your theme and its inheritance chain. If that
is missing,
.BR paplay (1)
or
.BR pw\-play (1)
play the freedesktop file directly: no theming, but not silence either.
.SH SCREEN SHARING
glassosd implements the notification server's inhibition API \(em
.BR Inhibit ,
.B UnInhibit
and the
.B Inhibited
property, the KDE extension to
.B org.freedesktop.Notifications
\(em so anything that already asks Plasma for quiet gets it here too.
.PP
.B xdg\-desktop\-portal\-kde
inhibits notifications while the screen is being cast, which means they stop
appearing mid\-share without glassosd having to watch for screen sharing itself.
Screen recorders and presentation tools using the same call work for the same
reason. There is no setting: an application explicitly asking for quiet is
honoured.
.PP
Inhibited notifications are suppressed on screen and still recorded, the same
as Do Not Disturb. The point is that nobody watching your screen sees them, not
that you lose them.
.PP
An inhibition belongs to the bus name that asked for it and is released when
that name goes away, so an application crashing mid\-share cannot leave
notifications silenced for the rest of the session.
.PP
Inhibition and Do Not Disturb are tracked separately \(em one is the user's
choice, the other an application's request \(em so turning Do Not Disturb off
does not cancel a screen recording's inhibition.
.PP
.nf
gdbus call \-\-session \-d org.freedesktop.Notifications \e
  \-o /org/freedesktop/Notifications \e
  \-m org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties.Get \e
  org.freedesktop.Notifications Inhibited
.fi
.SH QUIET WHILE BUSY
.nf
glassosdctl quiet\-while\-busy on
.fi
.PP
Notifications are held back while an application is asking that the screen not
blank \(em a video playing, a game, a presentation \(em and are still recorded,
the same as Do Not Disturb.
.PP
.B This is not fullscreen detection.
On Wayland a client cannot see another window's state, by design, and KWin
implements no foreign\-toplevel protocol, which is why dunst reports it cannot
do fullscreen detection here either. What is available is the signal
applications already send through
.BR org.freedesktop.PowerManagement.Inhibit ,
and it is arguably the better question: whether an application says the user is
watching something is closer to what a notification daemon wants to know than
whether a window is the size of the screen. A long download can hold the screen
awake without being fullscreen, and a fullscreen text editor holds nothing.
.PP
Measured rather than assumed: PowerDevil takes about five seconds to register
an inhibition, so a notification arriving in the first few seconds of a video
still appears. Nothing polls \(em the state is change\-signalled \(em the delay
is upstream.
.PP
This is a third, separate reason to stay quiet, alongside Do Not Disturb and an
application's own inhibition, so turning any one of them off does not cancel
the others.
.SH WIDGETS
The control centre's contents are a list, using swaync's widget names so a
migrated config reads the same way.
.PP
.nf
glassosdctl widgets "title,mpris,search,notifications,volume,backlight,buttons\-grid"
glassosdctl widgets default
.fi
.TP
.B title
The heading and the Clear button.
.TP
.B mpris
Media player: art, title, artist, transport controls.
.TP
.B volume
Volume slider; click the icon to mute.
.TP
.B dnd
Do Not Disturb toggle row.
.TP
.B search
Filters history as you type, matching app name, summary and body,
case\-insensitively. Escape clears it; Escape again closes the centre. Searching
is a whole\-history question, so typing while drilled into one application
clears that filter rather than searching only that application while appearing
to search everything.
.TP
.B notifications
The grouped list, its empty state, and the per\-app settings panel.
.TP
.B backlight
Brightness slider; needs
.BR brightnessctl (1).
.TP
.B buttons\-grid
The quick\-action grid.
.PP
Order in the list is the order on screen, and anything omitted is not built,
.B notifications
included.
.PP
That is why there is one special case. Before this key was an order it was a
plain set, and no such list ever named
.BR notifications ,
so reading an old config literally would produce a centre with no notifications
in it \(em which would look like a bad bug rather than a setting. A list that
does not name
.B notifications
is therefore read the old way: the built\-in order, filtered to what was asked
for, with
.B title
and
.B notifications
kept regardless. Naming
.B notifications
is how you opt in to ordering.
.B glassosdctl widgets
reports which of the two you are getting.
.SH THE BUTTON GRID
Buttons are
.BI "[Button " name ]
groups.
.PP
.nf
[Button dnd]
Icon=dnd
Label=Do Not Disturb
Span=2
Action=toggle\-dnd
Order=0
.fi
.TP
.B Icon
A bundled name, or any icon from your theme. Bundled:
.BR wifi ,
.BR bluetooth ,
.BR dnd ,
.BR settings ,
.BR lock ,
.BR power ,
.BR reboot ,
.BR media ,
.BR brightness ,
.BR volume\-high ,
.BR volume\-muted ,
.BR mic\-on ,
.BR mic\-muted .
.TP
.B Label
Text, or a Nerd Font glyph. Shown instead of an icon when no icon is set, and
alongside one when
.B Span
is greater than 1.
.TP
.B Span
How many grid columns this button occupies. Default 1. A spanning button shows
its glyph and its label together and drops its tooltip, having said what it is.
.TP
.B Tooltip
Shown on hover. The only place a single\-cell button's name survives.
.TP
.B Order
Sort position.
.TP
.B Command
Any shell command.
.TP
.B Action
One of the built\-ins, which need no external program:
.BR toggle\-dnd ,
.BR toggle\-wifi " (via " nmcli ),
.BR toggle\-bluetooth " (via " rfkill ),
.BR toggle\-power\-saver " (via power\-profiles\-daemon),"
.BR clear\-all ,
.BR lock ,
.BR reboot ,
.BR poweroff ,
.BR logout .
.PP
Every
.B toggle\-
action renders as a toggle showing live state rather than as a plain button,
and the state is read fresh each time the grid is drawn, so it stays right when
something else changes it.
.PP
Prefer these to a
.B Command
that opens a settings page. A quick action should do the thing:
.B plasma\-open\-settings kcm_networkmanagement
starts the whole System Settings shell, which takes seconds, where
.B toggle\-wifi
is immediate.
.SH THEMES
glassosd does not parse CSS. Its surfaces are QML, which has no CSS engine, and
pretending otherwise would mean shipping a fake one. What it has instead is a
JSON theme file covering the part of a swaync
.I style.css
people actually edit: the
.B @define\-color
block, the radii, the shadows and the font.
.PP
.nf
glassosdctl themes
glassosdctl theme\-file nord
glassosdctl edit\-theme
.fi
.PP
Themes live in
.IR ~/.config/glassosd/themes/NAME.json ,
which overrides
.IR ~/.local/share/glassosd/themes/ ,
which overrides
.IR /usr/share/glassosd/themes/ .
Saving the file re\-themes the running daemon: no restart, no reload command.
.PP
Every value is looked up in your theme first and falls back to the built\-in, so
a theme file only needs the keys you want to change. A two\-line theme is a
valid theme.
.SS Colours
Accepting
.BR #rgb ,
.BR #rrggbb ,
.BR #rrggbbaa ,
or
.B rgba(r, g, b, a)
exactly as written in a swaync stylesheet.
.TP
.B accent
Progress fill, active toggles, focus, the lock\-on chip.
.TP
.BR text.primary ", " text.muted ", " text.secondary
Summaries and headings; the app\-name line; body copy and timestamps.
.TP
.BR osd.background ", " panel.background ", " card.background
The OSD surface, the control centre sheet, the popup surface.
.TP
.BR entry.background ", " entry.backgroundHover ", " entry.edge
Rows inside the control centre, hovered, and the hairline between them.
.TP
.B surface.solid
Tooltips and other fully opaque surfaces.
.TP
.BR glass.edge ", " glass.sheen ", " card.sheen ", " edge.outer
The lit hairline around a glass surface, its top\-edge highlight, the same on a
solid card, and the outer border on non\-glass surfaces.
.TP
.BR control.fill ", " control.fillHover ", " control.fillActive ", " control.edge
Buttons, toggles and grid cells: idle, hovered, pressed, and their border.
.TP
.B slider.track
The unfilled part of a slider or progress bar.
.TP
.B chip.idle
An icon container in its inactive state.
.TP
.BR card.stackEdge ", " card.stackSeam
The peeking edges of a grouped stack.
.TP
.B urgency.critical
The critical\-urgency accent.
.TP
.B shadow.color
Drop\-shadow colour.
.SS Numbers
.TP
.B radius.card
corner radius on cards and popups. Default 16.
.TP
.B radius.chipRatio
icon-container roundness; 0.5 is a circle. Default 0.32.
.TP
.B radius.pill
whether small controls are fully rounded. Default true.
.TP
.B edge.width
border thickness. Default 1.
.TP
.B spacing.padding
padding inside surfaces. Default 12.
.TP
.B spacing.gap
gap between elements. Default 13.
.TP
.B spacing.cardGap
gap between stacked popups. Default 8.
.TP
.B spacing.screenMargin
distance from the screen edge. Default 10.
.TP
.B spacing.osdTopMargin
how far down the OSD sits. Default 118.
.TP
.B size.icon
icon size. Default 21.
.TP
.B size.notifyIcon
notification icon size. Default 30.
.TP
.B size.centreWidth
control centre width. Default 400.
.TP
.B level.segments
blocks in the segmented indicator. Default 16.
.TP
.B stack.depth
sheets showing behind a grouped card. Default 2.
.TP
.B stack.offset
how far each sheet peeks below the one in front. Default 9.
.TP
.B stack.inset
how far each sheet is drawn in at the sides. Default 8.
.TP
.B stack.falloff
taper applied to each successive peek. Default 0.78.
.TP
.B stack.radius
corner radius on the sheets. Default radius.card.
.TP
.B shadow.opacity
shadow strength. Default 0.48.
.TP
.B shadow.blur
shadow softness. Default 40.
.TP
.B shadow.offsetY
shadow drop distance. Default 8.
.TP
.B shadow.pad
room reserved for the shadow to render into. Default 42.
.TP
.B blur.saturation
backdrop saturation; 1.0 is off. Default 1.8.
.TP
.B blur.contrast
backdrop contrast, KWin only. Default 0.32.
.TP
.B blur.intensity
backdrop intensity, KWin only. Default 0.92.
.TP
.B motion.in
arrival duration, ms. Default 225.
.TP
.B motion.out
dismissal duration, ms. Default 195.
.TP
.B motion.rise
how far a card rises as it arrives. Default 14.
.TP
.B font.family
typeface. Default Noto Sans.
.TP
.B font.size
base point size. Default 11.
.PP
.B shadow.pad
must cover
.B shadow.blur
plus
.BR shadow.offsetY ,
or the falloff is clipped against the surface edge and the shadow looks weak
rather than soft.
.SS A minimal theme
.nf
{
  "accent": "#ff7043",
  "card.background": "#1a1a1a",
  "radius.card": 4
}
.fi
.SS Referencing other keys
A value starting with
.B @
resolves to another key, the way
.B @define\-color
values get reused in a stylesheet:
.PP
.nf
{
  "accent": "#88c0d0",
  "control.edge": "@glass.edge",
  "slider.track": "@control.fill"
}
.fi
.SS Making it not look like glass
Set opaque surfaces and turn the sheen off.
.I material\-dark.json
and
.I nord.json
both do this and are worth reading as worked examples.
.PP
.nf
{
  "card.background": "#211f26",
  "panel.background": "#1d1b20",
  "glass.sheen": "rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)",
  "card.sheen": "rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)",
  "blur.saturation": 1.0
}
.fi
.PP
There is also a one\-shot escape hatch needing no theme file:
.B glassosdctl solidity 1.0
pushes every glass surface toward opaque.
.SS If a theme does not load
A malformed file is reported with the exact byte offset, and the daemon falls
back to the next theme in the search path rather than starting unstyled. Watch
for it with
.BR "journalctl \-\-user \-u glassosd \-f" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/glassosdrc
This file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/glassosd/themes/
Themes, highest priority.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/glassosd/themes/
Themes, per user.
.TP
.I /usr/share/glassosd/themes/
Themes, shipped.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/glassosd/history.json
Notification history.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/glassosd/snoozed.json
Snoozed notifications and their wake times.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR glassosd (1),
.BR glassosdctl (1)
